To read frequency with an ATmega16 microcontroller in C, you can leverage its 16-bit Timer/Counter1, which includes the Input Capture functionality. I'll provide you with an example demonstrating how to use Timer/Counter1 to measure frequency.

For the example, let's assume you have a frequency signal connected to the Input Capture Pin, ICP1 (PD4 or PORTD.4, Pin 18 on ATmega16).

**Note**: For a better understanding of the example, consult the ATmega16 datasheet to reference the registers and descriptions, which is available at: https://www.ece.uc.edu/~omearac/5320/AVR%20Documents/AtMega16_Datasheets.pdf

1. Configure Timer/Counter1 registers and initialize the Input Capture function of ATmega16.

```c
#define F_CPU 8000000UL // Define your CPU frequency, e.g., 8MHz
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

volatile uint32_t captured_frequency;

void timer1_init()
{
    TCCR1A = 0; // Set Timer/Counter1 Control Register A to 0
    TCCR1B |= (1 << CS11) | (1 << ICES1); // Set the prescaler to 8, and capture on rising edge
    TIMSK |= (1 << TICIE1); // Enable Input Capture Interrupt for Timer/Counter1
    sei(); // Enable global interrupts
}
```

2. Write the ISR for the Input Capture Interrupt.

```c
uint16_t icp_previous = 0;
uint16_t icp_current = 0;

ISR(TIMER1_CAPT_vect)
{
    icp_previous = icp_current;
    icp_current = ICR1;

    captured_frequency = F_CPU / 8UL / (icp_current - icp_previous);
}
```

3. Initialize the timer and set up the USART for debugging, then check the `captured_frequency` variable in the `main` function loop. Here's an example setup:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

